            <ArticleTitle>Elevated soluble receptor for advanced glycation end product levels in patients with acute coronary syndrome and positive cardiac troponin I.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>590-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MCA.0b013e32834c751f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">High levels of soluble receptor for advanced glycation end products (sRAGE) have been shown to have an atheroprotective role; however, no data are available on this molecule in acute coronary syndromes (ACS). We evaluated sRAGE levels in patients with non-ST segment elevation ACS (NSTE-ACS) or with chronic stable angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studied 265 patients, 190 of whom had NSTE-ACS and 75 had chronic stable angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Plasma sRAGE values were comparable in the two groups (P=0.19). However, in the patients with NSTE-ACS, sRAGE levels were significantly higher in patients with cardiac troponin-I (cTnI) of more than or equal to 0.04 µg/l compared with those with cTnI of less than 0.04 µg/l [758 (493-1536 ) pg/ml vs. 454 (167-899) pg/ml; P=0.0037]. A significant correlation (r=0.323, P=0.0045) was found between sRAGE and cTnI levels in patients with NSTE-ACS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Plasma sRAGE levels are elevated in patients with NSTE-ACS with positive cTnI, suggesting that they could be related to myocardial cell damage.</AbstractText>
